This term refers to four specific contraceptive methods: intrauterine contraceptive devices (IUCDs), the intrauterine system (IUS or Mirena), progesterone injections and implants. These methods are particularly important as they are highly effective and do not affect fertility long term. In addition, they are useful for those who have compliance problems with other methods. NICE recommends that these methods should be discussed with all women who ask for information about contraception (NICE 2005).

